A||N||6||shawnster84||tie rod end problems||||1||
Z||000000||shawnster84||09-02-2001||10:01 AM||||My Car was making a loud banging noise on the front passenger side yesterday so I thought the wheel must have came loose..I took it home and it looks like the inner tie rod is loose..has anyone ever replaced one of these before?? the haynes manual only shows how to replace the outer one..thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||09-02-2001||10:46 AM||||It's not too tough. You will need 2 'special tools' to do the job. If you live near an Auto Zone, take advantage of their tool rental program.<BR>One is a GM adapter which fits over the slotted end next to the steering rack and the other the inner tie rod end slips inside (it's a long sleve) and mates up with the first part. Follow the instructions as necessary for the outer end first to remove it, replace the inner and reinstall the outer.<BR>A front end alignment is recommended.||||reg||1
Z||000002||shawnster84||09-02-2001||01:52 PM||||Now you got me confused, I just tried to get the rack off the car and the only thing holding it on is the part that goes to the steering wheel, how do you get it to unhook? I guess when I get pass this problem I'll know what tools your talking about..Thanks anyway though..||||reg||5
Z||000003||Seanh||09-02-2001||03:01 PM||||Now I don't feel so bad thinking I was the only one who had that problem. When I did my front end rebuild I got to the steering rack removal part about 1:30 in the morning, and it was all ready to come off at about 1:40, except the part your talking about. We pulled, twisted, yanked, beat with a BFH, oiled and pulled some more and it still wouldn't come off, mean while its 4:30 am. I went to bed, and posted this question the next morning and was told to drive a flat head screwdriver or wedge in the coupling that the coupling bolt was holding together. I went out and did that, and it fell out on the floor without problem. And to think that I fought that thing for 3 hours the previous night and it came out in less then 1 minute. Oh well, thats how ya do it...<P><BR>Somethings I thought I should add: This is assuming you did remove the coupling bolt right? I found it most convient to remove the spare tire pod to access it. (I changed mine a total of 3 times before I was happy, so yeah, it came in REAL convient to remove it for that. <p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||09-02-2001||10:45 PM||||If you want to replace an inner tie rod, why are you trying to remove the rack?||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||09-02-2001||11:09 PM||||You dont need to remove the rack to get the ends off and you dont need those special tools. I just did one. Turn it al the way to one side after removing the boot you will see that it unscrews from the rack gear. two big wrenches is all you need.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||09-03-2001||06:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B>You dont need to remove the rack to get the ends off and you dont need those special tools. I just did one. Turn it al the way to one side after removing the boot you will see that it unscrews from the rack gear. two big wrenches is all you need.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Oh Man!<BR>You just gave a way the secret!<BR>||||reg||1
